---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/meta';

const title = 'Install Mulchie on macOS or Linux';
const description =
  'Install Mulchie with Homebrew: the signed macOS menu bar app, or the CLI for macOS and Linux. Also the install script, Cargo, checksums and uninstall.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} ogImage="default" current="install">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Install Mulchie</h1>
    <p class="lede">Mulchie is a macOS menu bar app and a <code>mulchie</code> command for macOS and Linux. Install either with one command.</p>

    <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>CLI, engine, local API and MCP server: macOS or Linux (x86_64 or aarch64). Windows is not supported.</li>
      <li>Menu bar app: macOS 12 or later, Apple silicon or Intel.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="paths">Choose an install path</h2>
    <p>The menu bar app includes the <code>mulchie</code> command, so install the app or the CLI, not both.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">You want</th><th scope="col">Use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The menu bar app and dashboard on a Mac</td><td><a href="#app">Homebrew cask or the DMG</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The CLI on a Mac or on Linux with Homebrew</td><td><a href="#homebrew">Homebrew</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The CLI on a server, or without Homebrew</td><td><a href="#script">Install script</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>The CLI built from source</td><td><a href="#cargo">Cargo</a> or <a href="#source">build from source</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="app">The menu bar app</h2>
    <pre><code>brew install --cask williamrogersdev/tap/mulchie</code></pre>
    <p>This installs <code>Mulchie.app</code> in <code>/Applications</code> and links the <code>mulchie</code> command that ships inside it. The app is a universal build, signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple. Update with <code>brew upgrade --cask mulchie</code> and remove with <code>brew uninstall --cask mulchie</code>.</p>
    <p>Without Homebrew, download <code>Mulchie_&lt;version&gt;_universal.dmg</code> from the <a href={`${REPO}/releases`}>releases page</a>, open it and drag Mulchie to Applications. To put the command on your <code>PATH</code>, open the dashboard, choose <strong>Agent API</strong>, then <strong>Link mulchie into ~/.local/bin</strong>. This build does not update itself: download the newer DMG to update.</p>
    <p>To build the app from source instead, see <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>CONTRIBUTING</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="homebrew">Homebrew (CLI)</h2>
    <pre><code>brew install williamrogersdev/tap/mulchie-cli</code></pre>
    <p>This installs the <code>mulchie</code> command from the release tarball for your OS and CPU, after Homebrew checks its SHA-256. It works on macOS and on Linux with Homebrew. Update with <code>brew upgrade mulchie-cli</code> and remove with <code>brew uninstall mulchie-cli</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="script">Install script (CLI)</h2>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://mulchie.com/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
    <p>The script picks the release tarball for your OS and CPU, downloads it with its <code>.sha256</code> file, and installs nothing if the checksum does not match. Because it fetches the file itself, macOS does not quarantine it (see <a href="#gatekeeper">macOS and Gatekeeper</a>). It installs to <code>~/.local/bin/mulchie</code> and needs no <code>sudo</code>. To read it first, run <code>curl -fsSL https://mulchie.com/install.sh | less</code>, or open <a href="/install.sh">/install.sh</a>. The same script is in the repository, so you can also run it from GitHub:</p>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/williamrogersdev/mulchie/main/scripts/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
    <p>It accepts the same two variables as the command above.</p>
    <p>Two environment variables change what it does:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>MULCHIE_VERSION</code></td><td>The version to install, such as <code>0.3.0</code> or <code>v0.3.0</code>. The default is the latest release.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MULCHIE_INSTALL_DIR</code></td><td>Where to put the binary. The default is <code>$HOME/.local/bin</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://mulchie.com/install.sh | MULCHIE_VERSION=0.3.0 sh</code></pre>
    <p>If the install directory is not on your <code>PATH</code>, the script prints the line to add.</p>

    <h2 id="cargo">Cargo (CLI, from source)</h2>
    <pre><code>cargo install --git https://github.com/williamrogersdev/mulchie mulchie</code></pre>
    <p>This compiles the CLI from the repository. It needs a current stable Rust toolchain. Mulchie is not published on crates.io yet, so <code>cargo install mulchie</code> does not work.</p>

    <h2 id="source">Build from source</h2>
    <p>To build only the CLI from a checkout:</p>
    <pre><code>git clone {REPO}.git
cd mulchie
cargo install --path crates/mulchie-cli</code></pre>

    <h2 id="gatekeeper">macOS and Gatekeeper</h2>
    <p>The menu bar app and its DMG are signed with a Developer ID and notarized, so they open without a warning. The standalone macOS CLI binaries in the tarballs are ad-hoc signed, not Developer ID signed or notarized yet. In practice:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Installing the CLI with Homebrew or the install script works. The script downloads the file itself, so macOS does not quarantine it.</li>
      <li>A tarball you download with a browser is quarantined, and Gatekeeper blocks the binary when you run it. Extract it, then clear the flag:</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>xattr -d com.apple.quarantine ./mulchie</code></pre>

    <h2 id="first-run">First run</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Start the engine with <code>mulchie serve</code>, or at login with <code>mulchie service install</code> (see <a href="/docs/headless/">running headless</a>). If you installed the menu bar app, open it instead: it adds an icon to the menu bar and starts the engine.</li>
      <li>Make sure the install directory (<code>~/.local/bin</code> for the script) is on your <code>PATH</code>.</li>
      <li>Run <code>mulchie status</code> in a terminal. It uses the running engine and says so on stderr if it falls back to a local look.</li>
      <li>Check the setup with <code>mulchie doctor</code>. It prints pass, warn or fail for each check and changes nothing.</li>
      <li>Connect your agents with <code>mulchie mcp install --client claude-code</code> (or <code>claude-desktop</code>, <code>cursor</code>, <code>codex</code>). See <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP</a>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Mulchie scans folders such as <code>~/Developer</code>, <code>~/Projects</code> and <code>~/code</code> by default. Change them in Settings or in <a href="/docs/configuration/">the config file</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="verify">Verify a download</h2>
    <p>Each CLI tarball on the release page has a matching <code>.sha256</code> file, and the release has a <code>SHA256SUMS</code> file that lists every tarball. The install script checks the SHA-256 for you. To check by hand, download the files into one folder and run:</p>
    <pre><code>shasum -a 256 -c mulchie-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz.sha256</code></pre>
    <p>On Linux, use <code>sha256sum -c</code> with the same file. <code>SHA256SUMS</code> lists the files in the format that <code>sha256sum -c</code> reads.</p>

    <h2 id="update">Update</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Homebrew CLI: <code>brew upgrade mulchie-cli</code>.</li>
      <li>Install script: run it again.</li>
      <li>Cargo: run the <code>cargo install --git</code> command again with <code>--force</code>.</li>
      <li>Menu bar app: <code>brew upgrade --cask mulchie</code>, or download the newer DMG.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The engine also checks for newer versions of Mulchie and of the agent tools it finds, and reports them in <code>mulchie updates</code>. It installs nothing. See <a href="/docs/updates/">update checks</a> for what it contacts and how to turn it off.</p>

    <h2 id="uninstall">Uninstall</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Stop the engine (quit the menu bar app if you use it). If you set up a background service, run <code>mulchie service uninstall</code> first (see <a href="/docs/headless/">running headless</a>). If you connected an agent, run <code>mulchie mcp uninstall --client &lt;app&gt;</code> and, if you installed hooks, <code>mulchie hooks uninstall --client &lt;app&gt;</code> for each one.</li>
      <li>Remove the program: run <code>brew uninstall --cask mulchie</code> or <code>brew uninstall mulchie-cli</code> (Homebrew), delete <code>~/.local/bin/mulchie</code> (install script), run <code>cargo uninstall mulchie</code> (Cargo), or delete the app from Applications.</li>
      <li>Optional: delete <code>~/.config/mulchie</code>. It holds your config, API tokens, action log, session activity records and <strong>worktree backups</strong>. Keep the <code>backups</code> folder until you are sure you do not need any patch in it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Source and issues live on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
